From 62871da65945e4a987996dd978c7fb8b68380e9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dennis Berger <dbgh@knownhosts.org>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 00:25:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] refactor(engine): Extract the settlement decisions into a
 SettlementPolicy (no-changelog)

`StepSettledHandler` took two decisions from the step rows itself: which
successors a settlement decides, and whether the execution owes no further
step. Both now come from a `SettlementPolicy`, the handler's optional seventh
constructor argument.

`defaultSettlementPolicy` is the code that was in the handler. It calls
`decisionKeys`, `decideSuccessors` and `countExpectedSettledSteps` in the same
order, with the same store reads. `settlement.ts`, `completion.ts`,
`loop-ledger.ts`, `iteration-mapping.ts` and `graph/loops.ts` do not change.

A policy reads rows through a `SettlementReader`. The reader is bound to one
execution and has only the three planning reads:
`loadLatestStepSummaries`, `loadStepSummariesByKeys` and `countSettledSteps`.
It has no write method. `settlementReaderFor` passes each read through to the
`StepStore`, with no cache.

The failure branch, `hasFailedSteps`, `createSteps`, the announcements and
`cancelPendingSteps` stay in the handler. A failed execution does not ask the
policy.

No caller passes a policy, so the behaviour does not change. The existing
tests are not edited. `settlement-policy.test.ts` checks that the default
policy gives the same answers as direct calls, and that the handler uses an
injected policy.
